A||N||4||FieroJET||Temp sensors on same circut?||||5||
Z||000000||FieroJET||07-12-2001||06:54 PM||||My 86 GT has suddenly started overheating when travelling below 50 MPH even if thermostat is removed.  The temp light, temp gauge and temp fan switch have all stopped working.  Are they on the same circut or seperate?  Can't find anything obviously wrong.  I flushed and refilled the system using proper burping proceedure.  I know its overheating because of an aftermarket temp gauge installed on its own circut.  Thanks for any help.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Cliff Pennock||07-12-2001||08:26 PM||||If it's overheating fairly quickly (like within 10 minutes of a cold start) it's probably your waterpump.<P>Did you do check the fuses?||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJET||07-12-2001||11:38 PM||||I just put a new one in last year(water pump).  Could it have gone bad already?  It was a signature series from Advance and I made sure to get the one with the metal impeller.  It warms up to 180 within 5 -8 min. but instantly starts climbing the moment my speed falls or I stop.  It goes up and stays between 220-240 untill I get going again or turn on the A/C.  Its only 75 out and its never ran this hot.  Will say that this started after replacing the heads with 3.4's (2.8 block). Fan temp switch started working today still dont know whats up with the temp gauge & light.   Thanks as always||||reg||5
Z||000003||Cliff Pennock||07-13-2001||05:17 AM||||Read this:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001206-2-004064.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Archives/Archive-000001/HTML/20001206-2-004064.html</A> <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||07-13-2001||12:47 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroJET:<BR><B>...instantly starts climbing the moment my speed falls or I stop.  It goes up and stays between 220-240 untill I get going again or turn on the A/C...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <BR>Sure makes one think the radiator fan isn't always coming on when it should, doesn't it?  I don't know if the temp. switches get intermittent before they fail completely, but  I would ground the (green/white stripe) wire to the temp. switch temporarily and see if that alleviates the overheating.<P>Practically no-cost test..  a place to start.<BR>After that, I'd see if the fan relay isn't getting juice for some reason...<P>Oh, and the temp. gage and warning light - check to make sure the connector for the sending unit (aft left corner of rear head), hasn't cooked and crumbled off.  It happens.<P>Or maybe the 3.4 heads don't have the same sending unit?  Or, maybe the connector is just hanging there unconnected?  Forgot to plug it in when the heads were changed?<P>Sorry if you already checked these - I like to start with the simple, cheap stuff and go from there   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 07-13-2001).]||||reg||3
